\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \rtlch \af0\afs20\alang1037 \ltrch \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid407078 Tu B\rquote 
shevat, the day we celebrate the New Year for trees will take place this year on January 22. The Torah states, "ki ha'adam eitz hasadeh" -- "Man is like the tree of the field" (Devarim 20:19). The prophets compare the Jewish people to various trees. I
n what way is man likened to a tree and what lesson can we learn from this?
\par 
\par The spiritual growth process of a Jew is similar in many ways to the natural growth process of a tree. A tree is planted by first putting a seed in the ground. It is necessary to 
constantly water the ground to provide nourishment for the tree, and to remove all weeds to prevent the nourishment that is provided for the tree to be lost. In each and every Jew, Hashem planted a divine seed -- his soul. When a Jewish child is born, it 
is our obligation to nourish its soul, to water it with Torah study. We must also protect it by weeding out bad influences such as bad friends etc.
\par 
\par To assure that a young tree will grow straight, it is tied to two supports, one on each side. Similarly, to 
ensure that a young child grows beautifully, the parents must always be at his side and constantly supervise him and support him, ensuring that the child grows straight and tall in the service of our Creator. Even a little extra care given to a young seed
l
ing is greatly amplified and richly rewarded when the tree matures, and can make all the difference in the outcome of the tree. So too is extra care in the guidance of a young child. This, after all, is the crucial period in a child's formative years, whe
n the parents at home have an opportunity that will never be repeated. Any positive influence parents have on their small children will be amplified more and more in the years to come.
\par 
\par The strength of the tree depends on how well it is rooted in the ground
. The deeper the roots, the stronger the tree. A deeply rooted tree can withstand the elements and even a very strong wind will not shake it. The root of the Jew is his emunah, his faith in G-d. The more deeply rooted our emunah, the stronger we will be a
s Jews. A Jew with deeply rooted emunah can withstand the elements: he will not deviate from the path of goodness and holiness even when confronted with peer pressure and other outside influences.
\par 
\par To carry the analogy further, a tree attains fulfillment when it produces good fruit. The beauty of a tree is the fruit it produces. Mitzvot and ma'asim tovim -- good deeds -- are man's fruits. The more one does, the more beautiful a Jew one is.
\par 
\par Furthermore, good fruit is not merely good in itself -- but also con
tains the seeds to produce new trees and fruits after its kind, to the end of time. Similarly, it is within the ability of every Jew to set a good example for others, that they may also act in a G-dly way, performing Mitzvot and ma'asim tovim. They in tur
n will influence others in a chain reaction of great magnitude. Nevertheless, because this is the result of the original tree that behaved as it should all the good deeds are credited to the original tree.
\par 
\par This is how every Jewish boy and girl should be ra
ised and educated: Certainly to bring forth fruit, at the very least, but this is not enough, for their fruits -- their good influence -- must be ultimately felt to the end of the world and to the end of time.
\par 
\par Such an achievement seems rather a lot to expect of a limited human being. But actually it is well within reach, since a Jew operates with a Divine soul, a part of G-dliness Above, and operates with Torah and mitzvot given by G-d.
\par 
\par As we celebrate the New Year for trees, this year and every year, on the 15}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \super\insrsid407078 th}{\rtlch \af0 \ltrch \insrsid407078 
 day of Shevat, we would do well to keep these lessons in mind. We must see to it that we educate our children and guide them in a way that will guarantee the continuity of the Jewish People and the world in general.
\par }}